=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a one-sided administrative shutdown of leaf_router_0_0's eth1
interface (172.16.0.9/31), which is the physical link connecting it to
spine_router_0_1:eth1 (172.16.0.8/31). The interface carries the `<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>`
flag set with no `UP` flag, confirming it was brought down via
`ip link set eth1 down` rather than by a physical cable failure. FRR on
leaf_router_0_0 records exactly one link-down event timestamped
2026/08/04 18:16:40.67 and zero link-up events, corroborating that the
interface was downed and never recovered.

When eth1 went down, the directly connected route to 172.16.0.8/31 was
withdrawn from the kernel FIB. FRR's BGP daemon detected the loss of next-hop
reachability, reset the single established session to spine_router_0_1
(logged as "No path to specified Neighbor"), and entered Active state. The
session retry timer (120 s) fires repeatedly but every attempt fails for the
same reason — there is no egress path to 172.16.0.8 while eth1 is down.
Traffic between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 has not been impacted because
leaf_router_0_0 retains its session to spine_router_0_0 (eth0 is up) and
BGP ECMP/multipath is not required for basic reachability. On spine_router_0_1,
the session appears Established only because BGP Graceful Restart helper mode
is preserving the stale routes for up to 360 seconds, pending reconnection.
spine_router_0_1:eth1 is fully operational and has no fault.

Root cause: eth1 on leaf_router_0_0 was administratively shut down at the OS
level, removing the only path for the BGP session toward spine_router_0_1.

Corrective fix: Run `ip link set eth1 up` on leaf_router_0_0. The interface
will re-join the link (spine_router_0_1:eth1 is already UP), the connected
route to 172.16.0.8/31 will be reinstalled, and the BGP session will
re-establish automatically within the configured connect-retry interval.
=== END ===
